<p>Denmark’s snap parliamentary election left Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen’s Social Democrats as the largest party, but still short of a parliamentary majority, setting up what could be weeks of coalition talks in Copenhagen. With all votes counted, the left-leaning red bloc won 84 seats in the 179-seat Folketing, well below the 90 needed for a majority. The right-leaning blue bloc took 77 seats, while the centrist Moderates won 14 and now look set to play an influential role in forming the next government. The result was a mixed one for Frederiksen. Her party finished first again, leaving her in the strongest position to seek a third term, but it also posted its weakest result in more than a century after losing ground from the 2022 election. The campaign unfolded after tensions with President Trump over Greenland raised Frederiksen’s international profile, but Danish voters were also focused on domestic issues such as migration and welfare. That leaves the next government likely to depend on cross-bloc bargaining rather than any clear electoral mandate.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/jHi17n_g6eHuChRzv-rRpKRGgu_FZSFbJJ35cjmV7W_kObzAbnJHMX2d3nwLcG3GfwNELI7outXITDNULOXe9noWRoktR9QxFy4arwnMie-Q8DgkK5fa27JgfDbfKggGhZaxVsQlI6kZbjc5yjw4QVdVZ1hST71oEMydQ233MhZvnYEhN2UNBbVN5kzKm_nVHpBbasMgmynpKw3aFOJbskBx19-ZP6buq8L_TxCzR5Yu3iOi1VaLswYW43jOGsgitD6oyzh8107JOFjPSw' alt='Mette Frederiksen, Denmark&#x27;s Prime Minister and leader of the Social Democrats party, attends an election gathering after exit polls in Copenhagen on March 25, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Vote-share slump: The Social Democrats still finished first, with nearly 22% of the vote, but the result underscored how much support the party has lost even as Denmark’s biggest political force.</li><li>Coalition complication: Official results showed that both of Frederiksen’s partners in the outgoing government also lost ground compared with 2022, making a simple rerun of the previous coalition harder.</li><li>European pattern: Politico cast the outcome as part of a broader European trend in which center-left parties have recently shown resilience rather than collapse, even when their vote share slips.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Mette Frederiksen: Frederiksen said she intends to remain prime minister and acknowledged that forming the next government will be difficult. She added that she had already contacted several party leaders after the result. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/denmark-election-2026-survivor-mette-frederiksen-dealmaking-path-to-power/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Lars Løkke Rasmussen and the Moderates: The centrist Moderates entered the post-election talks holding the balance of power, putting Rasmussen in a position to decide whether to back another Frederiksen-led administration or a broader cross-bloc arrangement. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/danish-pm-s-left-wing-bloc-wins-vote-but-with-no-majority/a-76498223'>Deutsche Welle</a>)</li><li>Financial Times assessment: Frederiksen’s path to another term remained open in part because her main right-wing rival also fared poorly, leaving no obvious alternative claimant to power. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/e20aeb81-9b39-4f14-9e0b-57977c60bc9c'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Straits Times framing: The Social Democrats campaigned on a promise of stability in a turbulent world, an argument that helped the party remain the largest even after a bruising result. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/danish-premiers-party-wins-vote-as-tough-government-talks-await'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-denmark-election-social-democrats-seats/'>Denmark’s Social Democrats poised to lose seats after calling early election</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/danish-prime-ministers-future-unclear-after-election-results-show-indecisive-outcome'>Danish prime minister&#x27;s future unclear after election results show indecisive outcome</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3347777/danish-pm-mette-frederiksens-left-wing-bloc-wins-election-falls-short-majority'>Denmark faces weeks of coalition talks as Frederiksen emerges bruised in election</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/24/mette-frederiksen-leftwing-bloc-fails-win-majority-denmark-election-exit-poll'>Mette Frederiksen’s leftwing bloc fails to win majority in Danish election</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reddit.com/r/inthenews/comments/1s2vvto/danish_ruling_social_democrats_win_election/'>Danish Ruling Social Democrats Win Election Shaped by Trump</a> - reddit.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/danish-pm-s-left-wing-bloc-wins-vote-but-with-no-majority/a-76498223'>Danish PM&#x27;s left-wing bloc wins vote but with no majority</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/denmark-election-2026-survivor-mette-frederiksen-dealmaking-path-to-power/'>Survivor, Denmark: Mette Frederiksen clings on — offering lessons for Europe’s left</a> - politico.eu</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/danish-election-produces-inconclusive-result-leaves-prime-ministers-future-unclear/article70782850.ece'>Danish election produces inconclusive result, leaves Prime Minister&#x27;s future unclear</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/24/danish-pm-s-left-wing-bloc-leads-election-but-no-majority_6751788_4.html'>Danish PM Mette Frederiksen&#x27;s Social Democrats lead election but fall short of majority</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/24/world/europe/denmark-elections-mette-frederiksen.html'>After Standing Up to Trump Over Greenland, Denmark’s Prime Minister Leads in Election</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/eu